  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA deep trough is in place across the Great Basin, with southeast \nflow building across the central High Plains. Our CWA is still\nunder the influence of westerly flow aloft, with an embedded \nshortwave in SW flow to the south of the region. Surface ridging\nis in place and drier north and eventually northeast BL flow is\nundercutting the northern edge of this initial precipitation \nand while a few sprinkles may sneak into the south tonight, most\nlocations are expected to remain dry through midday Monday. \nAfter the more organized system moves through the region Monday \nafternoon through Tuesday, southwest flow develops and persists \nat least through the end of the week and potentially beyond the \nweekend (some variance in clusters between ridging and SW by \nthis weekend). The general trend should be for seasonal \ntemperatures to warm slightly from seasonal highs in the \n40s/lower 50s to slightly above average highs in the mid \n50s/near 60. SW flow aloft inherently brings the potential for \nadditional chances for precipitation, however due to the weak \nnature of the upper flow in the Plains and variance in guidance \nresolving any embedded waves confidence in measurable \nprecipitation/impacts is low beyond Tuesday. \n\n\n...Rain Monday into Tuesday...\n\nA stronger mid level wave organizes into a hybrid type system\nMonday  with large scale ascent increasing across our CWA south\nto north late Monday afternoon through Tuesday. Initially drier\nair in northeast BL flow may delay measurable rainfall, however\nthe surge of deeper moisture with WAA and at least some 700MB \ndeformation banding will help saturate the low levels and \nperiods of moderate (locally heavy) rainfall would be expected. \nAs is usually the case with dominant WAA patterns, stronger \nareas of frontogenesis will tend to be more transient making \ncoverage of higher totals 1\"+ harder to anticipate. Better \ncoverage of rain will tend to initially be along and south of US\nHighway 2, though the track of the mid/upper low will \neventually allow rain to overspread northwest/north central MN. \nHigh 24hr probabilities (60-80%) for 0.25\" or more rainfall are \nshown by NBM across southeast ND and west central MN, with a \n30-50% chance in similar areas for greater than 1\" of rainfall.\n\nRegarding snowfall: As with previous rounds of guidance the \ntemperature profiles may at times be cold enough to support a\nmix or change over to snow, particularly in higher terrain \nareas west of the Red River Valley late Monday night/early \nTuesday morning. However, as HREF/REFS/NBM all show a very weak \nsignal, with lighter rates the chance for any snow to accumulate\nwill be diminished due to warmer ground temperatures. Still, a \nfew novelty snowflakes or a light dusting on grassy/elevated \nsurfaces may occur (best chance west of the Red River Valley \n(no impacts).",
